const openEvidence=(key,label)=>`<button class="research-jump" data-research-tab="${key}">${label} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button>`;
const takeaway=(question,answer,limit)=>`<section class="result-reading" aria-label="Result explained"><span class="eyebrow">RESULT</span><h3>${question}</h3><p>${answer}</p><p class="reading-limit">${limit}</p></section>`;
function problem(){
 const dueFirst=exampleOrder==='due-first';
 return `<div class="research-heading problem-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">THE PROBLEM / START WITH ONE PUMP</span><h2>Factory scheduling</h2><p class="problem-statement">Choose <strong>who gets each resource next</strong> so customer orders reach shipping, with less lateness and cost.</p></div></div>
 ${problemWalkthrough()}
 <div class="definition-strip"><span><strong>Make the parts</strong>Housing + rotor + flange</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span><strong>Share the factory</strong>Machines, people, tools and robots</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span><strong>Deliver the pump</strong>Assemble → inspect → ship</span></div>
 <p class="problem-scope-line">The full synthetic benchmark targets <strong>24 initial orders over 96 hours</strong>. The factory viewer can load different runs. Each replay reports its own workload, shipments and checker scope.</p>
 <section class="definition-section"><span class="eyebrow">TRY ONE SCHEDULING DECISION</span><h3>One inspector. Two pumps. Which goes first?</h3><p>Illustration only: it is 09:00. Two otherwise finished pumps need one shared inspector before immediate shipment. Pump A needs 30 minutes and is due at 10:00. Pump B needs 20 minutes and is due at 09:40.</p><div class="example-switch" role="group" aria-label="Choose inspection order"><button data-example-order="a-first" aria-pressed="${!dueFirst}">Inspect A first</button><button data-example-order="due-first" aria-pressed="${dueFirst}">Inspect B first</button></div><div class="inspection-example">${inspectionTimeline(dueFirst)}<p class="example-outcome" aria-live="polite"><strong>${dueFirst?'Both ship on time.':'Pump B ships 10 minutes late.'}</strong><span>Both orders finish by 09:50 in either sequence. ${dueFirst?'Prioritizing B avoids a missed due date.':'Finishing all the work equally fast does not ensure on-time delivery.'}</span></p></div><p class="definition-aside">A part must be made and transported before inspection.</p></section>
 <div class="problem-next"><div><span class="eyebrow">FACTORY REPLAY</span><h3>Parts to shipping.</h3><p>Six steps from the loaded trace.</p></div><a href="./?tour=1">Open walkthrough ↗</a></div>
 <details class="reading-details problem-details"><summary>What is given, and what do we decide?</summary> <section class="definition-section"><span class="eyebrow">01 / THE INPUT IS FIXED</span><h3>We have orders to fulfill and resources to share.</h3><p>For the nominal case, <strong>24 customer orders each request one pump</strong>. Each has a release time (when we may begin) and a due time (when it should be delivered). We simulate <strong>96 hours</strong>; due dates can fall before that cutoff.</p><dl class="problem-inputs"><div><dt>For each pump</dt><dd>Make its housing, rotor and flange through their prescribed processing steps. Bring all three accepted parts together, assemble them, inspect the pump, then move it to shipping.</dd></div><div><dt>The factory</dt><dd>15 machines, 10 workers with specific skills and shifts, 4 battery-powered transport robots, shared tools and limited storage. Processing takes time and consumes resources and power.</dd></div><div><dt>Events during the run</dt><dd>Machines can become unavailable, inspections can fail, deliveries can be delayed, and urgent orders can arrive. The controller learns an outcome when it is revealed.</dd></div></dl><p class="definition-aside">These are synthetic benchmark inputs that we designed for Stage 2. Changing the workload, machine capacities, time rules or available equipment changes the problem instance.</p></section> <section class="definition-section"><span class="eyebrow">02 / THE UNKNOWNS ARE OUR DECISIONS</span><h3>Which task should get each scarce resource next?</h3><p>Two parts may both need the same mill. Only one can use it first. Several finished parts may need the same robot. A pump may be waiting for just one missing component. We must choose an order of work and assign times and resources.</p><div class="decision-columns"><div><strong>Production decisions</strong><p>Which orders to start, which ready part to process next, on which eligible machine, at what time and in which allowed mode.</p></div><div><strong>Supporting decisions</strong><p>Which worker and tool to assign, when and where to move parts, when to charge or service resources, and whether to request the allowed extra calibration kit.</p></div></div><p><strong>A solution must produce an executable schedule:</strong> records of operations and movements with start/end times and assigned resources, ending in actual shipments. A list of priorities alone is useful only if the controller can turn it into that schedule.</p></section></details>
 <details class="reading-details problem-details"><summary>What makes a schedule valid, and how do we score it?</summary> <section class="definition-section"><span class="eyebrow">04 / THE RULES A SCHEDULE MUST OBEY</span><h3>A plan must be physically possible.</h3><ul class="constraint-list"><li><strong>Correct order of steps.</strong> Finish and transport a part before its next operation; assemble only the three matching accepted components.</li><li><strong>No double booking.</strong> A machine, worker, tool or robot cannot perform incompatible jobs at the same time. Workers must be qualified and on shift.</li><li><strong>Real movement and storage.</strong> Parts must travel between locations. Buffers, routes, robot batteries and chargers have limits.</li><li><strong>Respect operating limits.</strong> Obey power availability, machine/tool wear, service requirements, quality rules and the allowed investment budget.</li><li><strong>Use only available information.</strong> React to failures and urgent orders after they are revealed. Future random outcomes are hidden.</li></ul></section> <section class="definition-section"><span class="eyebrow">05 / HOW WE COMPARE TWO SOLUTIONS</span><h3>First deliver more orders. Then deliver them less late. Then spend less.</h3><ol class="objective-order"><li><strong>Minimize orders still unshipped at hour 96.</strong><span>A component or an assembled pump still waiting for inspection or transport does not count as a shipment. Urgent orders count after they arrive.</span></li><li><strong>With equal fulfillment, minimize deadline debt through the cutoff.</strong><span>For each order: max(0, shipment time − due time). For an unshipped order, use the cutoff instead of shipment time. Add across orders.</span></li><li><strong>With equal fulfillment and lateness, minimize reported total cost.</strong><span>The load experiment uses cost as its final tie-break, including the extra kit’s rental cost.</span></li></ol><p>Because events vary, compare each controller across the same set of scenarios and rank their <strong>average</strong> outcomes in that order. Passing the physical checker is a prerequisite; an invalid schedule cannot win by reporting a better score.</p><p class="definition-aside">This is the ranking behind the retained load experiment. The broader design also proposes a penalty for unusually bad lateness scenarios; that risk-sensitive objective is a separate extension and is not measured by this grid’s ranking.</p></section></details>
 <details class="reading-details problem-details"><summary>What does our current experiment actually search?</summary> <section class="definition-section definition-search"><span class="eyebrow">06 / WHAT OUR CURRENT EXPERIMENT ACTUALLY SEARCHES</span><h3>The full scheduling problem is larger than the search we have run.</h3><p>The retained load experiment tries <strong>48 predefined dispatch rules per load</strong>. Each rule chooses a limit on active orders, an eco/standard/turbo machining mode, and whether to rent the extra calibration kit. The existing controller then builds the detailed operation and movement schedule.</p><p>So the measured question is: <strong>“Which of these 48 rules works best in this simulator?”</strong> It is a restricted way to approach the full problem above. A poor result can mean the rule is weak, the resources are insufficient, or both; it does not by itself distinguish those causes.</p><p>A more general solver could choose a much wider range of schedules or adapt its decisions during a run. Its results would need their own comparison on the same factory inputs and scenarios.</p></section></details>
 <div class="definition-footer"><p><strong>Problem type:</strong> production scheduling with alternative machines, assembly, transport and uncertain events. The adaptive solution is a <em>policy</em>: a rule mapping the currently observed factory state to its next decisions.</p>${openEvidence('overview','Now read the results')}</div>`;
}
function overview(){
 const {best,runs}=summarizeGrid(bundle.data.grid),large=best.find(row=>row.orders===24),b=bundle.data.bounds,t=b.labor.tools.find(row=>row.kind==='calibration');
 const latest=summarizeTooling(bundle.data.tooling_validation).find(row=>row.orders===12),improved=latest?.valid?latest.variants[1]:null,baseline=latest?.valid?latest.variants[0]:null;
 return `<div class="research-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">RETAINED EXPERIMENTS / RESULTS IN CONTEXT</span><h2>Controller results</h2><p>These retained experiments are a fixed evidence snapshot. The factory viewer may show a newer or different run. Start with these four findings.</p></div></div>

 <div class="evidence-digest" aria-label="Four findings at a glance">
 <article><span class="digest-number">${improved?`${improved.complete}/${improved.runs}`:'—'}<small>runs ship all</small></span><div><small>12 INITIAL ORDERS / LATER PAIRED COHORT</small><h3>Stable tool homes ship more orders.</h3><p>${improved?`Shipments rose from ${baseline.shipped} to ${improved.shipped} of ${improved.orders} realized orders across the same ${improved.runs} scenarios. This validation cohort also helped select the policy; it is development evidence.`:'Invalid or unmatched cohort data: performance summary suppressed.'}</p></div>${openEvidence('frontier','Inspect the policy comparison')}</article>
 <article><span class="digest-number digest-open">${number(large.mean_unfulfilled,2)}<small>orders left</small></span><div><small>24 INITIAL ORDERS / EARLIER TRAINING GRID</small><h3>The nominal target is still unresolved.</h3><p>This is the best of the tested rules on these training scenarios; it is not a proof of impossibility.</p></div>${openEvidence('frontier','See the workload limit')}</article>
 <article><span class="digest-number">+${number(t.deficit_ticks*b.tick_minutes)}<small>min</small></span><div><small>24 ORDERS / INITIAL CALIBRATION KIT ONLY</small><h3>One kit lacks enough available time.</h3><p>A conditional capacity proof. Renting an extra kit changes the assumption.</p></div>${openEvidence('capacity','Understand the bottleneck')}</article>
 <article><span class="digest-number">3<small>bits</small></span><div><small>SEPARATE 3-JOB BASELINE / CLASSICAL SIMULATION</small><h3>A tiny quantum example favors valid choices.</h3><p>The full factory is not reduced to three bits. No hardware execution or speedup is demonstrated.</p></div>${openEvidence('quantum','Try the quantum example')}</article>
 </div>
 <details class="reading-details"><summary>Factory model and demonstrated scope</summary>
 <section class="scope-explainer"><span class="eyebrow">WHAT PROBLEM DID WE DEFINE?</span><h3>A team-designed extension, with a declared scope</h3><p>Stage 2 expands the organizer’s small scheduling baseline into a <strong>synthetic two-hall pump factory</strong>. Its parameters are modeling choices, not measurements from a real factory. The nominal target is <strong>24 initial orders over 96 hours</strong>, using 15 machines, 4 transport robots and 10 workers, with shared tools, finite buffers, energy limits, quality failures and disruptions.</p><p>The load experiment varies initial orders from ${best[0].orders} to ${best.at(-1).orders}, keeping a 96-hour window; urgent arrivals add to that load. Smaller cases help check and understand the model. They do not replace the 24-order target.</p><details><summary>Proposed scope versus demonstrated scope</summary><p>The broader design also calls for emergency procurement, a leased machine, a reachable initial work-in-progress state, committed rolling replanning and interruption/resume behavior. These are not established by the replay’s checker. A general proof that policies cannot use future information is also absent.</p><p>The load grid ranks static dispatch rules by mean unshipped orders, mean cutoff-censored deadline debt, then mean cost. The broader design’s risk-sensitive objective and complete global optimum are not established by that grid. Each evidence tab states its own numerical model and restrictions.</p></details></section>
 </details><details class="reading-details"><summary>Read what each experiment establishes</summary><section class="results-explainer"><span class="eyebrow">WHAT DO THE RESULTS ACTUALLY MEAN?</span><h3>Four tests answer four different questions</h3><div class="result-ledger">
 <article><div><small>FULL FACTORY / DEVELOPMENT COHORTS</small><h4>Better resource decisions push the frontier.</h4><p>${(()=>{const group=summarizeTooling(bundle.data.tooling_validation).find(row=>row.orders===12);if(!group.valid)return 'The later tool-home cohort contains invalid or unmatched rows; its performance summary is suppressed.';const [old,next]=group.variants;return `At 12 initial orders, assigning shared tools to stable home machines raised shipments from <strong>${old.shipped} to ${next.shipped} out of ${next.orders}</strong> on ${next.runs} paired validation scenarios. Runs shipping everything rose from ${old.complete} to ${next.complete}.`;})()}</p><p>The earlier ${number(runs)}-run training grid remains below as a separate experiment. At 24 orders, its best tested rule left ${number(large.mean_unfulfilled,2)} unshipped on average.</p><p class="reading-limit">Validation scenarios also helped select the newer policy. No final held-out result or full-factory optimum is established here.</p></div>${openEvidence('frontier','Explore policy progress')}</article>
 <article><div><small>24 ORDERS / INITIAL KIT ONLY</small><h4>One calibration kit is a provable bottleneck.</h4><p>Even an optimistic calculation needs ${number((t.minimum_processing_ticks+t.minimum_service_ticks)*b.tick_minutes)} kit-minutes but has only ${number(t.effective_capacity_ticks*b.tick_minutes)} available. That is a <strong>${number(t.deficit_ticks*b.tick_minutes)}-minute shortfall</strong>.</p><p class="reading-limit">The proof assumes no additional kit. Renting a kit changes that assumption, so this is not a proof that the investment-enabled factory is impossible.</p></div>${openEvidence('capacity','See the capacity argument')}</article>
 <article><div><small>TINY DETERMINISTIC FACTORIES</small><h4>We can measure heuristic mistakes on small cases.</h4><p>An exact solver proves the shortest completion time within each tiny test’s declared domain. Comparing our scheduling rule with it shows how much longer the rule takes.</p><p class="reading-limit">Those percentages belong to the tiny cases. The full factory’s distance from the best possible schedule is unknown.</p></div>${openEvidence('exact','Compare exact checks')}</article>
 <article><div><small>OFFICIAL 3-JOB BASELINE / SEPARATE DEMONSTRATION</small><h4>A simulated quantum circuit can favor good schedules.</h4><p>Classical proofs reduce the baseline to three binary ordering choices and a nonlinear decoder. The circuit increases the chance of sampling an order that finishes within its 8-hour deadline.</p><p class="reading-limit">This is a separate tiny instance. No large-factory compression, hardware run or quantum speed advantage is demonstrated.</p></div>${openEvidence('quantum','Understand the quantum example')}</article></div></section>
 </details><div class="research-note">EVIDENCE SNAPSHOT · This page explains the retained experiments, not live progress from ongoing solver work. New methods or model revisions need separately labeled results. ${sourceLink('protocol')}</div>`;
}
function chart(){
 const grid=summarizeGrid(bundle.data.grid),{orders,rows,best}=grid;
 const w=1000,h=310,left=62,right=28,top=25,bottom=49,max=metric==='completion'?1:Math.max(...rows.map(row=>value(row,metric)),.1)*1.07;
 const x=order=>left+(order-orders[0])/(orders.at(-1)-orders[0])*(w-left-right),y=v=>h-bottom-v/max*(h-top-bottom);
 return `<svg class="frontier-plot" viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" role="group" aria-label="Training policy results across ${orders.length} initial-order loads; ${metrics[metric].name}">
 ${Array.from({length:5},(_,i)=>{const n=max*i/4;return `<path d="M${left} ${y(n)}H${w-right}" class="plot-grid"/><text x="${left-12}" y="${y(n)+3}" text-anchor="end">${metric==='completion'?percent(n):number(n)}</text>`;}).join('')}
 ${orders.map(order=>`<path d="M${x(order)} ${h-bottom}v5" class="plot-axis"/><text x="${x(order)}" y="${h-bottom+22}" text-anchor="middle">${order}</text>`).join('')}
 <text x="${w-right}" y="${h-5}" text-anchor="end">INITIAL ORDERS · URGENT ARRIVALS ADD TO THESE</text>
 ${rows.map(row=>`<circle cx="${x(row.orders)}" cy="${y(value(row,metric))}" r="${selected?.policy_id===row.policy_id&&selected.orders===row.orders?6:3}" class="policy-point ${selected?.policy_id===row.policy_id&&selected.orders===row.orders?'point-selected':''}" data-policy="${esc(row.policy_id)}" data-orders="${row.orders}" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="${esc(row.policy_id)}, ${row.orders} initial orders, ${number(value(row,metric),2)} ${metrics[metric].unit}"><title>${esc(row.policy_id)} / ${row.orders} initial orders: ${number(value(row,metric),2)} ${metrics[metric].unit}</title></circle>`).join('')}
 <polyline class="frontier-line" points="${best.map(row=>`${x(row.orders)},${y(value(row,metric))}`).join(' ')}"/>
 ${best.map(row=>`<circle class="best-point" cx="${x(row.orders)}" cy="${y(value(row,metric))}" r="5" pointer-events="none"/>`).join('')}
 </svg>`;
}
function policyDetails(row){return `<div class="policy-detail"><div><span class="eyebrow">SELECTED SCHEDULING RULE</span><h3>Up to ${row.weights.wip_limit} active orders · ${esc(row.weights.mode)} mode</h3><p>${row.weights.lease_calibration?'Rent an extra calibration kit.':'Use the initial calibration kit only.'} The active-order limit controls work in progress (WIP).</p><p class="policy-id">ID: ${esc(row.policy_id)}</p><p>${row.orders} initial orders · ${row.runs} training scenarios · ${row.valid}/${row.runs} passed physical checks</p></div><dl><div><dt>Orders shipped, average</dt><dd>${number(row.mean_shipped,3)}</dd></div><div><dt>Orders left unshipped, average</dt><dd>${number(row.mean_unfulfilled,3)}</dd></div><div><dt>Runs shipping every order</dt><dd>${row.complete_runs}/${row.runs}</dd></div><div><dt>Cutoff deadline debt, average per run</dt><dd>${number(row.mean_tardiness_minutes,2)} min</dd></div></dl></div>`;}
function recoveryDiagnostic(){
 const diagnostic=bundle.data.recovery_diagnostic,example=diagnostic.pairs.find(row=>row.seed===8),clock=t=>{const minutes=t*diagnostic.tick_minutes;return `${String(Math.floor(minutes/60)).padStart(2,'0')}:${String(minutes%60).padStart(2,'0')}`;};
 return `<section class="definition-section"><span class="eyebrow">SEPARATE DIAGNOSTIC / DELIBERATELY SELECTED FAILED TRAIN TAPES</span><h3>Move real parts to clear a path for one matched kit.</h3><p>This policy variant had already become blocked. The only toggle in each matched pair is physical recovery: same instance, workers, robots, buffers, tool homes, QA guard and admission rule. These four tapes were chosen because they failed; this is a mechanism demonstration, not a general performance estimate.</p>
 <div class="research-table-wrap"><table class="research-table"><caption>Selected failure diagnosis · no validation or final tapes</caption><thead><tr><th>TRAIN seed</th><th>Recovery OFF</th><th>Recovery ON</th><th>Realized orders</th><th>Still unshipped</th></tr></thead><tbody>${diagnostic.pairs.map(row=>`<tr><td>${row.seed}</td><td>${row.before.metrics.shipped}</td><td>${row.after.metrics.shipped}</td><td>${row.after.metrics.orders}</td><td>${row.after.metrics.orders-row.after.metrics.shipped}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>
 <p class="plot-caption">Every before/after trace passes a fresh physical check. Seed15 still misses two orders. Completing a selected failure does not establish a deadlock-free controller or success on unselected scenarios. This diagnostic uses a different policy variant from the validation cohort above.</p>
 <details class="feature-details"><summary>Follow the actual seed8 transport and assembly records</summary><div class="mechanism-chain">${example.actual_recovery_steps.map(step=>step.kind==='transport'?`<span><small>${clock(step.tick)} · ${esc(step.move_id)}</small><strong>${esc(step.origin)} → ${esc(step.destination)}</strong><br>${esc(step.unit_id)}<br>Pickup ${clock(step.pickup_tick)} · drop ${clock(step.drop_tick)}</span>`:step.kind==='retarget'?`<span><small>${clock(step.tick)} · OBSERVED STATE</small><strong>Retarget the staged plan</strong><br>Previous target ${esc(step.previous_order_id)}; this is not an assembly.</span>`:`<span><small>${clock(step.tick)} · ${esc(step.operation_id)}</small><strong>Start ${esc(step.order_id)} assembly</strong><br>${esc(step.machine_id)} · completes ${clock(step.end_tick)}<br>Consumes ${step.parent_unit_ids.length} original matching parents.</span>`).join('')}</div><p class="plot-caption">The donor component first moves between existing assembly buffers. Subsequent accepted parts move out of inspection into the freed space. The planner can retarget when a complete kit becomes observed; only the final linked assembly start consumes its original matched components. Every transport has actual robot timing and route legs in the trace. No resource or storage capacity is added.</p><p class="plot-caption">This is bounded classical search: at most ${example.recovery_policy.max_states} states and depth ${example.recovery_policy.max_depth}. Retargeted histories are intermediate staging, not successful assemblies.</p></details>
 <div class="research-note"><a href="?trace=./traces/recovery-train8-after.json&amp;compare=./traces/recovery-train8-before.json">Compare the two actual seed8 factory replays ↗</a> · A: recovery ON; B: OFF. Scores retain cutoff-censored debt separately from delivered-only tardiness. ${sourceLink('recovery_diagnostic')}</div></section>`;
}
function toolingProgress(){
 const groups=summarizeTooling(bundle.data.tooling_validation),load12=groups.find(row=>row.orders===12);
 if(!groups.every(row=>row.valid))return '<p class="research-note">Later policy comparison suppressed: invalid or unmatched cohort rows are retained in the source evidence.</p>';
 const [old,next]=load12.variants,replay=bundle.data.tooling_replay.fresh_independent_check.metrics;
 return `<div class="research-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">LATER EXPERIMENT / VALIDATION USED FOR SELECTION</span><h2>Tool placement</h2><p>Same physical factory, same paired scenarios. The controller restricts its tool assignments; the tools remain physically portable.</p></div></div>
 ${takeaway('Can a better policy make this complex factory handle more work?',`At load 12, shipments rise from ${old.shipped}/${old.orders} to ${next.shipped}/${next.orders}; ${next.complete}/${next.runs} scenarios ship every realized order. Urgent arrivals are included.`,`This cohort (seeds ${next.seeds[0]}–${next.seeds.at(-1)}) helped select the policy. It is development evidence, not untouched final evaluation. Two scenarios remain incomplete, and late shipments still count.`)}
 <div class="probability-plot">${load12.variants.map(row=>`<div><span>${row.variant==='off'?'Calendar baseline':'Stable tool homes'}</span><div><i style="width:${row.shipped/row.orders*100}%"></i></div><strong>${row.shipped}/${row.orders}</strong></div>`).join('')}</div>
 <p class="plot-caption">Total shipments across the same ${next.runs} scenarios. Physical tool relocations fell from ${number(old.relocations)} to ${number(next.relocations)}. This is an observed policy ablation, not a proof that each avoided move causes an additional shipment.</p>
 <div class="research-table-wrap"><table class="research-table"><caption>Later paired cohort · separate from the older training grid below</caption><thead><tr><th>Initial orders</th><th>Policy</th><th>Shipped / realized</th><th>Runs shipping all</th><th>Tool relocations</th></tr></thead><tbody>${groups.flatMap(group=>group.variants.map(row=>`<tr><td>${group.orders}</td><td>${row.variant==='off'?'Calendar baseline':'Stable tool homes'}</td><td>${row.shipped}/${row.orders}</td><td>${row.complete}/${row.runs}</td><td>${number(row.relocations)}</td></tr>`)).join('')}</tbody></table></div>
 <div class="research-two-col"><section><h3>A tool trip occupies more than the tool.</h3><p>Moving a shared kit also consumes transport time. Fixing preferred homes can remove repeated transfers, while restricting which eligible machines the controller uses.</p><p>The factory still checks tool location, life and service, worker skills, route travel, energy and finite buffers.</p></section><section><h3>A pump needs a whole matched kit.</h3><p>Assembly waits for its own accepted housing, rotor and flange. Filling buffers with parts from many incomplete orders can block the missing component's path.</p><p>The separate selected-failure diagnostic below tests actual physical buffer recovery. It is not pooled into this validation cohort.</p></section></div>
 <div class="research-note">TRAIN REPLAY · <a href="?trace=./traces/tool-homes-train0-12.json">Open the 12-order stress replay ↗</a> · Seed0 ships ${replay.delivered_orders}/${replay.delivered_orders+replay.unfulfilled_orders} realized orders: checked and ${replay.unfulfilled_orders?'incomplete':'complete'}. ${replay.operations} operations and ${replay.moves} moves are recorded. This single run is distinct from the validation cohort above. ${sourceLink('tooling_selection')} · ${sourceLink('tooling_validation')} · ${sourceLink('tooling_replay')}</div>`;
}
function frontier(){
 const grid=summarizeGrid(bundle.data.grid);if(!selected)selected=grid.best.find(row=>row.orders===8)??grid.best[0];
 const eight=grid.best.find(row=>row.orders===8),large=grid.best.find(row=>row.orders===24);
 return `${toolingProgress()}${recoveryDiagnostic()}${objectiveMechanism(bundle.data.objective_mechanism,sourceLink,bundle.data.reward_confirmation)}<div class="research-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">EARLIER EXPERIMENT / 96 HOURS / TRAINING SCENARIOS</span><h2>Original dispatch rules</h2><p>Try different initial order loads in the original grid. Each scheduling rule is run on ${eight.runs} random scenarios (seeds), which determine disruptions, quality outcomes and urgent arrivals.</p></div><div class="research-stamp"><strong>${number(grid.runs)}</strong><span>checked training simulations</span></div></div>
 ${takeaway('Shipping everything is possible at 8 initial orders; 24 remains unsolved by this grid.',`The selected rule at 8 ships every realized order in ${eight.complete_runs}/${eight.runs} training runs, but averages ${number(eight.mean_tardiness_minutes)} cutoff-censored late minutes. At 24, the selected rule leaves ${number(large.mean_unfulfilled,2)} orders unshipped on average.`,`This compares ${grid.rows.length/grid.orders.length} tested rules per load, not every possible schedule. All ${number(grid.valid)} runs pass the physical checker. That establishes checked validity, not on-time delivery, optimality or performance on unseen scenarios.`)}
 <div class="plot-toolbar"><label>Vertical axis <select id="research-metric">${Object.entries(metrics).map(([key,m])=>`<option value="${key}"${key===metric?' selected':''}>${m.name}</option>`).join('')}</select></label><span><i class="dot"></i>Every policy <i class="line"></i>Selected by training objective</span></div>
 <p class="metric-reading">${metrics[metric].explanation}</p>${chart()}<p class="plot-caption">Read left to right: more orders arrive at the start. Each dot is one scheduling rule’s average across the training scenarios. The line follows the rule selected at each load: first minimize unshipped orders, then cutoff-censored deadline debt, then cost. Changing the vertical axis keeps those same selected rules. Click a dot or a policy in the table to inspect it.</p>
 <div id="policy-detail">${policyDetails(selected)}</div>
 <div class="research-table-wrap"><table class="research-table"><caption>Selected rule at each load · averages include urgent orders</caption><thead><tr><th>Initial orders</th><th>Avg. shipped</th><th>Runs shipping all</th><th>Avg. unshipped ↓</th><th>Avg. cutoff debt ↓</th><th>Selected rule ID</th></tr></thead><tbody>${grid.best.map(row=>`<tr><td>${row.orders}</td><td>${number(row.mean_shipped,3)}</td><td>${row.complete_runs}/${row.runs}</td><td>${number(row.mean_unfulfilled,3)}</td><td>${number(row.mean_tardiness_minutes,2)} min</td><td><button data-policy="${esc(row.policy_id)}" data-orders="${row.orders}">${esc(row.policy_id)}</button></td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div><p class="plot-caption">Why can shipped orders exceed the initial load? Urgent orders can arrive during a run. For example, 9.5 shipped at load 8 is an average across scenarios with different realized totals. “Runs shipping all” includes those arrivals and allows late shipments.</p>
 <div class="research-note">TRAINING RESULT · No held-out generalization claim. ${sourceLink('grid')}</div>`;
}
function capacity(){
 const b=bundle.data.bounds,t=b.labor.tools.find(row=>row.kind==='calibration'),minutes=v=>v*b.tick_minutes;
 const cap=minutes(t.effective_capacity_ticks),proc=minutes(t.minimum_processing_ticks),service=minutes(t.minimum_service_ticks),required=proc+service,scale=900/required;
 return `<div class="research-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">NECESSARY BOUND / FIXED INITIAL CAPACITY</span><h2>Capacity bound</h2><p>${b.orders} nominal orders over ${number(b.horizon_ticks*b.tick_minutes/60)} hours. This proof excludes new leases, rework, shocks and urgent orders.</p></div></div>
 ${takeaway('Could perfect scheduling finish 24 orders with just the initial calibration kit?',`No, under these stated rules. Every pump requires inspections using this shared kit. Even the minimum processing and service time exceeds the kit’s usable worker-covered calendar by ${number(minutes(t.deficit_ticks))} minutes. Rearranging jobs cannot create that missing time.`,`The full model allows renting a second kit. This result identifies a capacity requirement; it does not prove that 24 orders remain impossible after investment, or that renting a kit is sufficient.`)}
 <div class="capacity-visual"><div class="capacity-label"><span>Unavoidable calibration work</span><strong>${number(required)} min</strong></div><svg viewBox="0 0 1000 135" role="img" aria-label="Required calibration processing and service exceeds worker-accessible capacity by ${minutes(t.deficit_ticks)} minutes"><rect x="35" y="30" width="${proc*scale}" height="32" class="capacity-process"/><rect x="${35+proc*scale}" y="30" width="${service*scale}" height="32" class="capacity-service"/><path d="M${35+cap*scale} 13v91" class="capacity-limit"/><text x="35" y="88">PROCESSING ${number(proc)} + SERVICE ${number(service)}</text><text x="${35+cap*scale}" y="123" text-anchor="end">ELIGIBLE-WORKER CALENDAR ${number(cap)} MIN</text></svg><div class="capacity-label"><span>Required − available</span><strong>+${number(minutes(t.deficit_ticks))} min</strong></div></div>
 <div class="research-two-col"><section><h3>What is proved</h3><p>The initial calibration kit needs at least ${number(proc)} minutes of processing and ${t.minimum_services} services totaling ${number(service)} minutes. Its QA/setup worker-calendar union permits ${number(cap)} usable minutes.</p><p>Synchronized breaks remove ${number(minutes(t.minimum_forced_idle_tool_ticks))} minutes. Service and calendar availability are bounded jointly, avoiding double-counting idle time.</p></section><section><h3>What changes the conclusion</h3><p>The full model permits an additional calibration kit lease. This certificate establishes infeasibility with the initial kit; it does not establish infeasibility of the investment-enabled factory.</p><p>A necessary bound passing is not a feasible schedule. Transport bounds also grant free initial battery charge and omit empty repositioning and congestion.</p></section></div>
 <div class="mechanism-chain" aria-label="Interacting factory mechanisms">${['Recipe + matched parts','Machines + blocking','Worker calendars','Mobile tools + service','AGV routes + energy','Quality + urgent arrivals','Capacity investment'].map((v,i)=>`<span><small>0${i+1}</small>${v}</span>`).join('')}</div>
 <div class="research-note">CONDITIONAL CERTIFICATE · Instance ${esc(b.instance_sha256.slice(0,16))}… · Full-model infeasibility proved: ${String(b.full_model_infeasibility_proven)}. ${sourceLink('bounds')}</div>`;
}
function multiReference(){
 const rows=bundle.data.multi_reference.rows;
 const four=rows.find(row=>row.pumps===4&&row.attendance==='automatic'),parallel=four.cp_runs.find(run=>run.workers>1),latest=four.cp_runs.find(run=>run.completed_closeout),extension=four.supplemental_bound_extension,cost=bundle.data.closeout_accounting,downloads=bundle.data.reference_two_hour_downloads;
 return `<section class="definition-section"><span class="eyebrow">PUSHING THE REFERENCE / TWO TO FOUR MATCHED PUMPS</span><h3>More work quickly becomes harder to prove optimal.</h3><p>Seven stations, one worker and one transport robot; every pump adds 16 operations and 19 mandatory part transfers. Processing, finite buffers, blocked outputs, matched assembly and physical loaded/empty travel remain. Quality, shifts, energy and service limits are deliberately nonbinding in this reference.</p>
 <div class="research-table-wrap"><table class="research-table"><caption>Completion minutes · strongest tested heuristic versus certified interval for the optimum</caption><thead><tr><th>Pumps / attendance</th><th>Heuristic</th><th>Best checked schedule</th><th>Lower bound</th><th>Heuristic gap</th><th>Proof status</th></tr></thead><tbody>${rows.map(row=>{const gap=row.heuristic_makespan_gap_interval;return `<tr><td>${row.pumps} / ${row.attendance==='full'?'full':'automatic'}</td><td>${number(row.development_heuristic.makespan_ticks)}</td><td>${number(row.best_checked_upper_bound_ticks)}</td><td>${number(row.best_certified_lower_bound_ticks)}</td><td>${row.optimum_proven?percent(gap.lower):`${percent(gap.lower)}–${percent(gap.upper)}`}</td><td class="reference-proof-status">${row.optimum_proven?'OPTIMAL':'Unresolved'}<small>${row.cp_runs.map(run=>`${run.budget_seconds}s / ${run.workers??1} CPU cap: ${run.status}`).join(' · ')}</small></td></tr>`;}).join('')}</tbody></table></div>
 ${parallel?`<p class="plot-caption">An earlier bounded Axion trial used ${parallel.workers} CPUs and improved the checked four-pump schedule from ${number(four.constructive_upper_bound_ticks)} to ${number(parallel.incumbent_ticks)} minutes. Its CP lower bound is ${number(parallel.cp_lower_bound_ticks)}, below the independent arithmetic bound ${number(extension.lower_bound_ticks)} retained from that stage. Slurm reports ${number(parallel.cost.scheduler_elapsed_seconds)} wall seconds and ${number(parallel.cost.allocated_cpu_hours,2)} allocated CPU-hours; no GPU use. It remains ${esc(parallel.status)}, not an optimum proof. Different hardware and compute budgets prevent a controlled parallel-speedup claim.</p>`:''}${latest?`<p class="plot-caption"><strong>2026-10-17 completed reference:</strong> a fresh ${latest.workers}-CPU search reports ${esc(latest.status)}, a ${number(latest.cp_lower_bound_ticks)}-minute solver lower bound and a ${number(latest.incumbent_ticks)}-minute checked upper witness: the remaining interval is [${number(four.best_certified_lower_bound_ticks)}, ${number(four.best_checked_upper_bound_ticks)}], not a proven optimum. Slurm records ${number(latest.cost.scheduler_elapsed_seconds)} elapsed seconds and ${number(latest.cost.allocated_cpu_hours,3)} allocated CPU-hours; process CPU is separately ${number(latest.cost.process_cpu_seconds,3)} seconds. No parallel-speedup claim. ${sourceLink('reference_two_hour_audit')}</p><p class="plot-caption">The raw CP trace passes physical checking but retains the stock identity auditor’s early-reservation rejection. A separate witness copy changes only <code>move74.start</code> from 1382 to canonical pump birth 1420; every physical leg, operation, pickup/drop and shipment time stays unchanged. That derived witness passes both unchanged physical and stock identity checks. The lower bound permits broader early reservations; it remains a relaxation bound for the stricter witness domain, without asserting identical optima. <a class="research-source" href="${esc(downloads.audit)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Audit + raw rejection ↗</a> · <a class="research-source" href="${esc(downloads.derived_witness)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Derived witness JSON ↗</a></p>`:''}${extension?`<p class="plot-caption">The separately retained guarded arithmetic proof raised the four-pump lower bound from ${number(extension.previous_lower_bound_ticks)} to ${number(extension.lower_bound_ticks)} minutes. Its prefix/hall-walk union argument retains all stated deterministic-fixture assumptions; ${extension.complete_witnesses_checked} matching retained witnesses pass the accompanying checks. It remains valid but is weaker than the completed solver bound. This is a supplemental mathematical certificate, not a revised CP output or an optimum proof. ${sourceLink('reference_bound_extension')}</p>`:''}<p class="plot-caption">The optimum lies between the lower bound and best checked complete schedule. A range is an interval for the heuristic's excess completion time, not an exact gap. “RUNNING” contributes no invented result. Automatic means selected manufacturing operations release the worker after start attendance; inspections, welding and assembly still reserve the worker throughout.</p>
 <div class="research-two-col"><section><h3>Retain unsuccessful searches too.</h3><p>The 600-second full-attendance searches for three and four pumps did worse than the stronger scheduling-rule grid. Those rules therefore remain the best checked witnesses in those rows.</p><p>The separate grid contains ${bundle.data.multi_reference.grid_valid} physically valid runs, including ${bundle.data.multi_reference.grid_incomplete} incomplete runs. Incomplete schedules are retained and cannot become upper bounds for complete fulfillment.</p></section><section><h3>A proof bound and a construction do different jobs.</h3><p>Concatenating two smaller checked schedules gives four automatic pumps a ${number(four.constructive_upper_bound_ticks)}-minute schedule, including the real empty robot return. ${parallel?`The earlier parallel trial reaches ${number(parallel.incumbent_ticks)}${latest?`, and the completed follow-up reaches ${number(latest.incumbent_ticks)}`:''}; all witnesses remain retained.`:'This is an upper bound, not a four-pump optimality proof.'}</p><p>Automatic attendance has an independently reviewed prefix bound: unavoidable initial manufacturing wait, later hall walks and final shipping add to mandatory tending. Full attendance separately counts all processing plus first delivery, hall walks and shipping. Original CP bounds remain preserved.</p></section></div>
 <div class="research-note">SAME-INSTANCE DEVELOPMENT / NO FLAGSHIP GAP · ${parallel?'Earlier local searches use one CPU; the separately labeled Axion trials use 16.':'Each search uses one CPU worker.'} Longer runs start fresh from checked hints, not a resumed proof state. ${sourceLink('multi_reference')}</div>${cost?`<p class="plot-caption">Research closeout as of ${esc(cost.as_of_utc)}: ${number(cost.research_finished_allocated_cpu_hours_including_failed,3)} allocated CPU-hours including failed jobs; ${cost.research_running_job_ids.length} running jobs, recorded GPU-hours ${cost.recorded_gpu_hours}, physical QPU calls ${cost.physical_qpu_calls}. Final-evaluation allocations are included in that total. Local setup/audits and agent inference are not a complete host-wide meter. This later accounting note is separate from the frozen final-evaluation export’s earlier as-of snapshot. ${sourceLink('closeout_accounting')}</p>`:''}</section>`;
}
